From 29c429bf3d001442f93379b7793b4749cd4e59c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ljhhkx529 <ljhzyx520@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:24:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 03/15] probe: a yellow band that shows how far the boot got

The board's boot produced a screen with no sign of how far it came, and the
serial log was the only place the answer was. This adds a fifth band to the
entry probe: a yellow strip the kernel paints when it reaches a chosen step,
so the panel itself says where the boot is.

The band is drawn through the kernel's own vinfo (v_baseaddr is the mapped
framebuffer), not the boot-argument physical address, because by the time the
later stages run the MMU is on. The stage it marks is selected by the caller,
and the one this commit leaves in place is the Data mount: stage 4.

The steps the band moves through are the ones the boot actually reaches:
AppleImage4 registering its API, PE_create_console returning with the vinfo set
up, the bl _bsd_init call site, NativeUfs::probe attaching the root, the
RamBlock attaching the Data volume, the volume lookup and the seed, the
persistent store attaching and being read, the Data mount call and its
completion. Each step moved the band one place, so the trail is the history of
the bring-up itself.

The payload builder grows the matching switch: a board that asks for
paint_yellow = true gets the probe patched with --paint-yellow.
---
 scripts/patch-xnu-entry-probe.py | 103 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 scripts/unixnu/payload.py        |   5 +-
 2 files changed, 105 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/scripts/patch-xnu-entry-probe.py b/scripts/patch-xnu-entry-probe.py
